UID:11fc72c04ae7d6ea@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200359Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Trump Administration issues guidance on healt
 h-contingent wellness programs\, including tobacco cessation programs
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor announced today th
 at its Employee Benefits Security Administration\, along with the departm
 ents of Health and Human Services and Treasury\, issued guidance related 
 to questions raised in dozens of class-action lawsuits challenging the tob
 acco surcharges some employers add to premiums for their group health plan
 s through workplace wellness programs. The Health Insurance Portability 
 and Accountability Act of 1996 and the Affordable Care Act allow employer
 s to reward employees for healthy habits through wellness programs. Wellne
 ss programs can cover things like nutrition\, weight loss\, tobacco cessat
 ion\, fitness\, alcohol and drug abuse assistance\, managing stress\, or g
 eneral health education.The FAQs released today announce the departments
 ’ approach to enforcement for health-contingent wellness programs\, whi
 ch are programs that reward an employee if they satisfy a standard related
  to a health factor. Until further guidance or regulations are issued\, th
 e departments will not take enforcement action against plans or issuers th
 at do not give employees the wellness program reward retroactively to the 
 beginning of the plan year after the employee completes a reasonable alter
 native standard. Under the terms of the enforcement relief in these FAQs\,
  plans and issuers only need to provide the reward prospectively\, from th
 e point where the employee completes the alternative standard\, if retroac
 tive rewards are not otherwise provided.The FAQs also address plans’ and
  issuers’ disclosure obligations related to health-contingent wellness p
 rograms. Specifically\, if plan materials merely mention that a health-con
 tingent wellness program is available\, without describing its terms\, the
  plan or issuer is not required to disclose the availability of a reasonab
 le alternative standard to qualify for the reward under the wellness progr
 am.“The experimental nature of wellness programs enables them to drive s
 ignificant and clinically meaningful health main\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsr
 oom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20260826
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20260826
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9a4fa702b1a03d33@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200302Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Bradbury Mountain State Park Selected as 2026
  Leave No Trace Spotlight\; Community Events Set for September 3-5
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nDACF Home\n→\nAbout DACF\n→\nNewsroom\n→ News
 \nBradbury Mountain State Park Selected as 2026 Leave No Trace Spotlight\;
  Community Events Set for September 3-5\nAugust 26\, 2026\nThree days of a
 ctivities will bring together Leave No Trace\, Maine State Parks\, volunte
 ers\, and community partners to celebrate and care for one of Maine's trea
 sured outdoor spaces.\nPownal - The Maine Department of Agriculture\, Cons
 ervation and Forestry (DACF) announced today that Bradbury Mountain State 
 Park in Pownal has been selected as a 2026 Leave No Trace Spotlight\, brin
 ging three days of community events\, outdoor education\, and hands-on ste
 wardship to the park and surrounding area September 3-5\, 2026.\n"Bradbury
  Mountain is a place people return to again and again\, to hike\, bike\, g
 ather with family\, take in the view\, and enjoy being outside\," said Chr
 is Silsbee\, Manager of Bradbury Mountain State Park. Being selected as a 
 Leave No Trace Spotlight gives us a great opportunity to bring the communi
 ty together\, have some fun\, and put stewardship into action. Whether you
  join us for trivia\, sit around the campfire\, or spend Saturday working 
 on a trail\, there's a way for everyone to be part of it.\nActivities begi
 n Thursday evening in Freeport and continue through Saturday at Bradbury M
 ountain State Park.\nSeptember 3\, 2026: Leave No Trace Trivia Night\nSept
 ember 4\, 2026: Fireside with the Ranger\, Leave No Trace Staff & Hot Dog 
 Roast\nSeptember 4\, 2026: Leave No Trace Outreach Tent\nSeptember 5\, 202
 6: Leave No Trace Event & Stewardship Project\nSeptember 5\, 2026: Leave N
 o Trace Stewardship Project\nThere is also a special incentive for early a
 rrivals: the first 25 cars through the park gate whose driver says\, "Bigf
 oot Leaves No Trace at Bradbury\," will receive a gift bag featuring items
  from local event sponsors.\nAbout the Leave No Trace Spotlight Program\nE
 ach year\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13360948
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13360948
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:48183e296f0fb7a0@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200223Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Safe Homes Awareness Month - September
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, the 130th Maine Legislature unanimously passed L.D
 . 1861\, An Act to Establish the Safe Homes Program\, and as Governor\, I 
 signed the bill into law on April 7\, 2022\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, this biparti
 san law promotes the safe storage of prescription drugs\, firearms and oth
 er dangerous weapons in homes or public spaces\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, the Safe
  Homes Program is an optional\, voluntary grant program that builds on the
  work of L.D. 1140\, An Act To Establish a Sales Tax Exemption for the Pur
 chase of Firearm Safety Devices\, by making storage devices\, such as gun 
 safes\, more affordable for people who want to buy them\; and\nWHEREAS\n\,
  the Safe Homes Program Fund will provide grants for purchasing safety dev
 ices for the safe storage of prescription drugs\, firearms\, and dangerous
  weapons in homes or public spaces\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, according to a repor
 t by the U.S. CDC\, there were 181 deaths by firearms in the State of Main
 e in 2024\, with over 86 percent of the deaths the result of suicide\; and
 \nWHEREAS\n\, in 2025\, there were 390 confirmed and suspected fatal drug 
 overdoses\, 20.4 percent lower than the total confirmed fatal overdoses fo
 r 2024\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, by ensuring that firearms and prescription drugs
  are properly stored and secured\, this law will reduce accidental gun dea
 ths\, prevent suicide\, and improve public health\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, in ac
 cordance with 1 MRSA § 150-R\, the month of September of each year is des
 ignated as Safe Homes Awareness Month to raise awareness of the importance
  of safety in the home and the assistance that is available through the Sa
 fe Homes Program established in Title 25\, section 7001\;\nNOW\, THEREFORE
 \n\, be it resolved that I\, Janet T. Mills\, Governor of the State of Mai
 ne\, do hereby proclaim September 2026 as\nSafe Homes Awareness Month\nthr
 oughout the State of Maine\, and I urge all citizens to recognize this obs
 ervance\, promote the proper storage of prescription drugs and firearms\, 
 and learn more about the assistance available through the Safe Homes Progr
 am.\nI\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamatio
 ns/2026-08-safe-homes-awareness-month-september
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-08-safe-homes-awareness-month-september
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:04599e5aa206db87@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200223Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Honey Bee Day -  September 13
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, honey bees (Apis Mellifera) and other native insec
 ts are essential pollinators that contribute to food security by pollinati
 ng the plants we use and one-third of the food we consume\; and\nWHEREAS\n
 \, honey bees face serious threats from many sources including pests and v
 iruses\, loss of habitat\, toxic pesticide and herbicide usage\, and chang
 es in climate and resulting water patterns\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, honey bees a
 re essential in the production of Maine's heritage crops including blueber
 ries and apples\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine's honey bees produce approximatel
 y 450\,000 pounds of honey each year and contribute an estimated annual $1
 00-150 million to the state’s economy\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine has almos
 t 2\,000 beekeepers ranging from hobbyists with as few as one hive\, to pa
 rt time business owners\, to full time commercial beekeepers with thousand
 s of hives\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, while traditional beekeeping occurred in rur
 al areas of Maine for the purpose of helping pollinate farm crops\, beekee
 ping is now growing in urban areas as the result of rising numbers of hobb
 y beekeepers interested in increasing biodiversity and local food producti
 on\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, the State of Maine has an exemplary apiary inspectio
 n program for the protection of honey bees and education of beekeepers in 
 the prevention and spread of apiary diseases and pests\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, 
 since 1975 the Honey Bee has been recognized as Maine's official state ins
 ect\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, the Maine State Beekeepers Association is celebrati
 ng its 50th year of incorporation in 2026\, and will host the annual “Be
 e Fest” on September 13 at the Viles Arboretum in Augusta\;\nNOW THEREFO
 RE\n\, be it resolved\, that I\, Janet T. Mills\, Governor of the State of
  Maine do hereby proclaim September 13\, 2026 as\nMaine Honey Bee Day\nand
  I encourage all Maine people to join in recognizing the importance of hon
 ey bees to our state.\nIn testimony whereof\, I have caused\nthe Great Sea
 l of the State to be\nhereunto affixed GIVEN under my\nhand at Augusta thi
 s tw\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations
 /2026-08-maine-honey-bee-day-september-13
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-08-maine-honey-bee-day-september-13
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d10ca78a1fa95adb@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200218Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Attorney General Aaron M. Frey Announces Larg
 est Big Tech Settlement in History
DESCRIPTION:FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nContact: Danna Hayes\,\nDanna.hayes@mai
 ne.gov\nAttorney General Aaron M. Frey Announces Largest Big Tech Settleme
 nt in History\nMeta to Pay Up to $17 Billion and Implement Sweeping Child-
 Safety Reforms on Instagram and Facebook\nAUGUSTA – Today\, Attorney Gen
 eral Aaron M. Frey announced a landmark $17.1 billion multistate settlemen
 t with Meta Platforms\, Inc.— the largest state consumer protection sett
 lement in history outside the Big Tobacco settlements of the 1990s. The se
 ttlement is subject to court approval. In addition to the payment\, Meta m
 ust implement a sweeping set of safety features designed to protect childr
 en on Instagram and Facebook. The agreement resolves claims by 47 States a
 nd D.C.\, Puerto Rico\, American Samoa\, and the Northern Mariana Islands 
 that the company designed Instagram with addictive features\, knowingly ex
 posed young users to serious mental harms\, and intentionally misled the p
 ublic about the safety of its platforms\, among other things. This settlem
 ent is a victory for the protection of America’s children and an importa
 nt first step to transform how the entire social media industry designs pr
 oducts for kids and teens. Under the settlement\, Maine will receive no le
 ss than $56 million.\n“This hard-fought settlement provides important pr
 otections for children on Meta’s platforms\, Instagram and Facebook\, an
 d empowers parents with tools to help address the negative impacts of soci
 al media use on children\,” said\nAttorney General Frey\n. “Notably\, 
 Meta is agreeing to measures designed to ensure that children under 13 are
  not on its platforms. While this settlement is an important step\, the ne
 ed to make social media safer for children must extend to the entire indus
 try. We will continue to work with our multistate partners to push for com
 prehensive changes that prioritize the safety and welfare of children over
  profit.”\nThe settlement requires Meta to implement a series of safety 
 features on Instagram and Facebook\, including:\nDaily ti\nhttps://www.mai
 ne.gov/ag/news-and-library/press-releases/attorney-general-aaron-m-frey-an
 nounces-largest-big-tech-settlement
URL:https://www.maine.gov/ag/news-and-library/press-releases/attorney-gene
 ral-aaron-m-frey-announces-largest-big-tech-settlement
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a418e47b2c2d9043@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200218Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Attorney General Aaron M. Frey Sues United St
 ates Postal Service Over Unlawful Attempt to Interfere with States’ Vote
 -by-Mail Procedures
DESCRIPTION:FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nContact: Danna Hayes\,\nDanna.hayes@mai
 ne.gov\nAttorney General Aaron M. Frey Sues United States Postal Service O
 ver Unlawful Attempt to Interfere with States’ Vote-by-Mail Procedures\n
 Lawsuit Seeks to Block Unconstitutional USPS Rule that Directs States to O
 verhaul Vote-by-Mail Procedures on an Impossible Timeline and Risks Disenf
 ranchising Eligible Voters\nAUGUSTA –\nAttorney General Aaron M. Frey to
 day joined a coalition of 25 states in filing a lawsuit against the United
  States Postal Service (USPS) challenging its new rule that purports to gr
 ant USPS the authority to regulate mail voting in elections.\nTens of mill
 ions of voters across the country rely on mail-in ballots\, and the coalit
 ion argues USPS’s rule will prevent states from effectively administerin
 g their mail voting programs\, putting voters at risk of being disenfranch
 ised.\n“USPS and the President are inflicting violence on free and fair 
 elections through this unlawful attempt to control voting by mail\, said\n
 Attorney General Frey\n. “This is not hard: the Constitution\ndoes not\n
 trust the Executive Branch with the responsibility of facilitating electio
 ns and this new rule – this election interference – must be stopped.
 ”\nOn March 31\, President Trump issued an Executive Order attempting to
  establish a national list of eligible voters and directing the U.S. Posta
 l Service to transmit mail ballots only to voters on the list. On August 2
 1\, USPS announced a new rule implementing the President’s demands that 
 require states to register every voter who is eligible to vote by mail wit
 h USPS and tasking USPS with compiling the registered voters into state-sp
 ecific lists. The rule also establishes new design requirements for the ma
 il-in ballot envelopes created by states\, which states are required to me
 et and get approved by USPS. Under the rule\, USPS will not deliver ballot
 s to voters who do not appear on the USPS-generated lists and will not del
 iver ballots in envelopes that fail to comply with the \nhttps://www.maine
 .gov/ag/news-and-library/press-releases/attorney-general-aaron-m-frey-sues
 -united-states-postal-service
URL:https://www.maine.gov/ag/news-and-library/press-releases/attorney-gene
 ral-aaron-m-frey-sues-united-states-postal-service
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
